# Australian Loyalty & Rewards Aggregation Landscape — Research Report for a Points-Tracking PWA

**Prepared:** May 2026
**Audience:** Founding team / product lead for a consumer points-tracking PWA targeting the Australian market

## TL;DR

- **The four programs to hardcode in v1** — Everyday Rewards, Flybuys, Qantas Frequent Flyer, and Velocity Frequent Flyer — all use round-number conversions that have been stable for years (Everyday Rewards and Flybuys both run on a "2,000 points = $10" model), but the cents-per-point *value* a user actually gets varies 5-10x depending on redemption choice. That gap (intrinsic conversion vs. realised value) is the single most defensible product wedge.
- **There is no realistic programmatic path** to balance data for any major Australian loyalty program in 2026 — no public APIs, no Consumer Data Right (CDR) coverage, T&Cs that prohibit credential sharing, and no Plaid-equivalent for loyalty. Manual entry + OCR is the only viable v1 path, and credential-based aggregation carries meaningful legal and account-termination risk. Plan the product around this constraint rather than around a roadmap that depends on it being lifted.
- **The competitive set in Australia is thin and content-led** (Point Hacks, Australian Frequent Flyer) rather than tool-led. AwardWallet works but has weak AU program coverage; Stocard was effectively wound down after its acquisition by Klarna (announced 7 July 2021). The gap to exploit is an Australian-first, opinionated **"what are my points actually worth and what should I do with them?"** product — not a generic loyalty-card wallet.

