## 3. Competitive Landscape

### 3.1 Direct competitors

**AwardWallet** (international)
- The dominant global player. Tracks 600+ programs worldwide via credential-based login.
- Pricing: Free tier (limited features, ads, no expiry tracking on every account) + **AwardWallet Plus at US$29.99/year** (per AwardWallet's own pricing page through 2024–2025).
- AU program coverage: weak. Qantas and Velocity have variable support; Everyday Rewards and Flybuys are not credential-aggregated. Most AU users use it for airline + hotel tracking only.
- Weakness against an AU-native product: doesn't speak AU sweet-spots, doesn't integrate AU supermarket programs, US-centric content.

**Stocard** (acquired by Klarna)
- Klarna announced the acquisition of Stocard on **7 July 2021** (per Klarna's press release of that date).
- Post-acquisition: pivoted into a payments/shopping app; the loyalty card-wallet functionality was deprecated / de-prioritised in many markets through 2022. The user experience deteriorated significantly post-2022 and Stocard is effectively no longer a real competitor in AU as a dedicated points tracker.

**Frequent Flyer Solutions (Australian)**
- Sydney-based. Their flagship product is **Award Flight Assist / Reward Flight Finder** — a tool that searches airline award availability for QFF and Velocity members. Not a points-tracking app per se; complementary product.
- Subscription model with a free tier and paid tier.

**Point Hacks (AU) and Australian Frequent Flyer**
- Content sites, not tools. Both publish points valuations, redemption guides, and sweet-spot articles. Point Hacks operates a credit card affiliate model. AFF runs a large community forum.
- Neither operates a points-tracking app.

### 3.2 International players who could enter

- **The Points Guy** (US) — has an Australian editorial presence (TPG Australia) but no app launched in AU. Their US app does some points-valuation work.
- **MaxRewards (US)** — focused on credit card rewards optimisation; not in AU.
- **Curve / Revolut / Wise** — payment cards with reward features but not points trackers.

### 3.3 Newer AU entrants (2023–2026)

This is the area with the thinnest direct sourcing in this report. *Flag for verification: do a targeted search of the AU App Store + Product Hunt + Crunchbase + SmartCompany for "Australian points tracker" launches 2023–2026 before going to market.* The current understanding is that this space has remained surprisingly empty — likely because of the data-access problem.

### 3.4 Gaps to exploit

1. **AU-native conversion data** — no current product treats Everyday Rewards and Flybuys as first-class.
2. **Cents-per-point estimation in AUD** — most global tools show "balance" but not "what's it worth in dollars today, given your typical redemption pattern".
3. **Sweet-spot recommendations specific to AU origins** — "you have X Velocity points, here are 3 redemptions that get you >3¢/pt out of SYD/MEL/BNE".
4. **Expiry alerts for AU programs** — QFF (18 months inactivity), Velocity (24 months inactivity), Everyday Rewards (12 months inactivity historically) — all have different rules and aggressive expiry. Verify each before shipping alert logic.
5. **Bonus point offer aggregation** — Everyday Rewards Boosts and Flybuys offers are valuable, time-limited, and currently scattered across email + app.

