## 4. Legal & Regulatory Considerations (Australia)

### 4.1 Privacy Act 1988

- Storing third-party login credentials (loyalty program usernames/passwords) on behalf of users brings the operator within scope of the Privacy Act as a regulated APP entity if turnover exceeds $3M (or other triggers apply, e.g. health service provider).
- Even below the threshold, holding credentials raises practical and reputational risk well above other PII categories.
- The **Privacy Legislation Amendment (Enforcement and Other Measures) Act 2022** raised the maximum penalty for serious or repeated interference with privacy under s 13G of the Privacy Act 1988 from AU$2.22 million to the **greater of: AU$50 million; three times the value of any benefit obtained through the misuse of information; or 30% of the entity's adjusted turnover during the relevant period.**
- The **Privacy and Other Legislation Amendment Act 2024 received Royal Assent on 10 December 2024**, enacting a first tranche of the Attorney-General's Department review recommendations — including enhanced OAIC enforcement powers and a new Children's Online Privacy Code obligation. Broader reforms (including a statutory tort for serious invasion of privacy) remained pending as of May 2026.

### 4.2 Australian Consumer Law (ACL)

- The ACL prohibits misleading conduct. Marketing a points tracker as "official" or "in partnership with [Program]" when it is not would be problematic.
- ACL does not specifically address scraping or aggregation; the issue is contract law (breach of program T&Cs) and potentially the **Copyright Act** if scraping copies substantial original expression.

### 4.3 Computer access offences

- The Criminal Code Act (Commonwealth) s 478.1 and equivalent state offences criminalise unauthorised access to computer data. Using a user's voluntarily-supplied credentials to access their own account is generally **not** an unauthorised access offence (the user authorised it), but the program operator may take a different view, and there are no settled Australian cases on aggregation specifically.
- No Australian equivalent of US **hiQ Labs v LinkedIn** has been decided on loyalty scraping.

### 4.4 ACCC Customer Loyalty Schemes Report — follow-up

- The ACCC's **"Customer Loyalty Schemes — Final Report"** was published on **17 December 2019** and identified, by name, concerns about opaque data practices, devaluations without notice, expiry traps, and limited consumer ability to compare programs.
- Many of the data-practice recommendations were folded into the ongoing Privacy Act reform process rather than producing standalone loyalty regulation.
- The ACCC has not (to my knowledge) brought a major enforcement action against a loyalty program operator since the report, though there has been continued attention to opaque pricing and bait advertising. *Verify with the current ACCC actions register.*

### 4.5 CDR & data portability

- Loyalty is not in CDR scope as of 2026 and there is no announced expansion. Treasury has signalled that CDR rollout has been slower and more expensive than expected, which makes a near-term expansion to loyalty unlikely.

