## 5. Technical Implementation

### 5.1 OCR for points-balance screenshots

| Option | Suitability for PWA | Cost | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Tesseract.js** | Excellent for client-side OCR in browser; ~10–15MB WASM bundle | Free (open source) | Accuracy is moderate; struggles with stylised digits and overlay graphics. Good enough for typed balance figures on clean backgrounds. |
| **Google ML Kit (Text Recognition)** | Best for native; via PWA you'd hit Cloud Vision instead | Native: free; Cloud Vision: ~AU$2.30 per 1,000 images after free tier | Highest accuracy. Cloud cost manageable if OCR is gated behind premium tier. |
| **AWS Textract** | Overkill for short numeric strings; designed for forms/tables | ~US$1.50 per 1,000 pages | Not recommended for simple balance reads. |
| **Apple Vision (VNRecognizeTextRequest)** | Native iOS only; not available to PWA directly | Free | If you build a native shell later, this is the iOS choice. |

**Recommendation:** Tesseract.js in v1 for free-tier users (privacy-friendly, on-device); Google Cloud Vision for premium-tier users when accuracy matters. Pre-process screenshots (crop to balance region, threshold, deskew) before OCR — this matters more than the engine choice.

### 5.2 Barcode / QR storage and display

- **Display** — for in-store scanning, render the user's loyalty barcode (typically Code 128 or QR) at high contrast, maximum brightness, locked screen orientation. The `bwip-js` library is the standard for browser-side barcode rendering.
- **Apple Wallet** — supports loyalty passes (PassKit, `.pkpass` format). PWAs can serve a `.pkpass` file via a download link; iOS Safari recognises the MIME type and offers "Add to Apple Wallet". You cannot programmatically push to Wallet from a PWA — user must tap. You'll need an Apple Developer account and pass signing.
- **Google Wallet** — supports loyalty cards via the Google Wallet API. A web-issued "Add to Google Wallet" button is supported. Requires Google Pay & Wallet console enrolment and class definitions per pass type.
- **PWA-only fallback** — render the barcode in-app, which works fine for most scanners (cashiers scan straight off the screen).

### 5.3 PWA capabilities in 2026 — current state vs native gap

- **iOS Safari Web Push** — supported since **iOS 16.4, which shipped on 27 March 2023** (per Apple's official release notes), but only for PWAs added to the Home Screen. Reliable but requires the user to "install" the PWA.
- **Camera access** — `getUserMedia()` works in iOS Safari and Chrome on Android. File input with `capture` attribute is the simplest path for OCR ("upload a screenshot").
- **Offline storage** — IndexedDB widely supported; Cache API for assets. Quotas are generous (typically hundreds of MB).
- **Background sync** — supported on Chrome/Android, **not on iOS Safari**. Periodic balance refresh in background is not viable on iOS.
- **Geolocation** — works on both.
- **Apple Wallet / Google Wallet integration** — works from PWA via standard MIME-typed downloads.

**The remaining gap with native:** background activity (refresh, scan), tighter biometric integration, and App Store discovery. None block a v1.

### 5.4 Suggested v1 architecture

- **Frontend:** React/Next.js PWA, service worker, IndexedDB for offline balances.
- **Backend:** Node/Express or serverless (Lambda/Cloud Functions); Postgres for user/program/balance entities; S3/GCS for screenshot blobs (encrypted at rest, short retention).
- **OCR:** Tesseract.js client-side default; Cloud Vision premium path.
- **Push:** VAPID for cross-browser web push.
- **Auth:** Email + magic link or social; treat loyalty credentials as a separate, deferred capability.
- **Hosting:** Vercel/Cloudflare Pages + AWS/GCP for backend; Sydney region for data residency given privacy considerations.

