## 6. Business Model & Pricing Benchmarks

### 6.1 Comparable pricing globally

- **AwardWallet Plus** — US$29.99/year (current listed price on AwardWallet's pricing page).
- **The Points Guy app** — free, ad/affiliate-funded.
- **MaxRewards Gold (US)** — ~US$50/year.
- **Australian fintech comparables** — Pocketbook (free, shut down), Frollo (B2B pivot), Up Bank (bank), WeMoney (free + premium ~AU$10/month).

### 6.2 Recommended AU pricing structure (initial)

| Tier | Price | Includes |
|---|---|---|
| Free | $0 | Up to 5 programs, manual entry only, basic balance + expiry tracking |
| Premium | AU$4.99/month or AU$39/year | Unlimited programs, OCR, cents-per-point valuations, sweet-spot recommendations, expiry push alerts, Apple/Google Wallet pass generation |
| Premium+ (later) | AU$9.99/month | Credential aggregation (where supported), redemption availability searching (QFF/Velocity classic rewards), family sharing |

### 6.3 Affiliate / referral revenue

- **Credit card affiliates** — the highest-revenue category in AU points media. Point Hacks' business model. Affiliate payouts of AU$50–$300 per approved card application are typical (verify with current affiliate networks: Commission Factory, Impact, direct).
- **Hotel bookings** — Hotels.com / Booking.com affiliate; modest per-booking commission.
- **Insurance, energy switching, foreign exchange** — secondary affiliates with reasonable AU CPA rates.

### 6.4 Willingness-to-pay

AU consumers pay for fitness (Strava ~AU$15/month), productivity (Notion, Todoist), and budgeting (YNAB ~AU$15/month, WeMoney ~AU$10). A points tracker at AU$4.99/month sits comfortably below these, justified if the value capture is "one extra business class redemption saves you thousands of dollars."

