## 7. Best-Redemption Logic — What's Actually True

### 7.1 Per-program "best value" defaults

| Program | Default "best value" framing for v1 |
|---|---|
| Everyday Rewards | $10 in-store; Qantas conversion only if user is QFF-engaged and pursuing a flight redemption goal |
| Flybuys | $10 in-store; Velocity conversion only if user is Velocity-engaged |
| Qantas Frequent Flyer | International business class on partner metal (Emirates, Qatar, Cathay); avoid Store unless points expiring |
| Velocity | Singapore Airlines / ANA / Qatar business class; avoid Store |
| Amex MR | Hold until you have a specific redemption; transfer ratios are mostly 2:1 to airlines, so 100k MR ≈ 50k Velocity |
| Hotel programs | Use for high cash-rate properties (resort, peak season) where cpp exceeds your valuation benchmark |
| QSR (MyMacca's etc.) | Always redeem at the highest-value tier you can reach; points have no other escape route |

### 7.2 Decision rules to encode

1. **If a point is within 60 days of expiry**, value-floor it at the *easiest* redemption (Pay with Points, Store) rather than holding.
2. **If a user transfers credit card points to an airline**, only flag it as a good move when the user has a specific Classic Reward / Reward Seat target identified — otherwise hold flexibility.
3. **Devaluation risk**: flag any balance > 100k in a single airline program as concentration risk, since major devaluations historically happen with limited notice.
4. **Earn velocity**: surface programs where the user is earning but not redeeming for > 12 months — these are the highest churn-risk balances.

### 7.3 Source basis

- Point Hacks publishes a periodic "Point Hacks Points Valuations" article — use as the primary anchor for cents-per-point figures, with attribution.
- Australian Frequent Flyer forum discussions for community-validated sweet spots.
- The Points Guy AU coverage is thinner than the US site but useful for partner-airline business class.

